Editing configuration.nix and running nixos-rebuild switch never touches
flake.lock at all. The lock only records input pins (your nixpkgs revision);
module edits don't change inputs, so switch just rebuilds against the same
pin.

nix flake update (not nixos flake update — it's a nix subcommand, run as
your normal user, no sudo needed since it's just writing a file you own)
does rewrite flake.lock — but only the local copy on whichever machine you
ran it on. There is no live/network sync between the two machines' lock
files. "Shared lock" describes the design (one file governs both hosts'
nixpkgs pin) — git is what actually keeps the two copies in sync, and only
when you move commits through it. So the real flow when you want to bump
nixpkgs:

  1. On one machine: cd /etc/nixos && nix flake update
     → rewrites flake.lock locally
  2. git add flake.lock && git commit && git push
  3. On the other machine: git pull --ff-only
     → now it has the same lock
  4. sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake /etc/nixos#<attr>
     on each machine, whenever you're ready to actually activate the new
     pin there

Until step 3 happens on the second machine, its flake.lock is stale relative
to the first — same as today, where git pull is what carried the
restructure over, not some automatic mechanism. If you skip pushing/pulling
and just run nix flake update independently on both machines, they'll
likely drift to different nixpkgs revisions (defeating the whole point of
combining them).

================================================================================

REFERENCE · NIXOS FLAKES
Dual-Host NixOS Workflow

How edits, rebuilds, and the shared flake.lock flow between your two
machines, both tracked through ~/pyohmine/nixos/flakes.

  PC — Hyprland   #nixos
  Proxmox VM      #vm_nixos

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1 · Edit a host's config                                    (either machine)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Each host only ever reads its own subdirectory — editing one never touches
the other. Hardware files are machine-detected; leave them alone.

  # on the PC
  $EDITOR /etc/nixos/hosts/nixos/configuration.nix

  # on the VM
  $EDITOR /etc/nixos/hosts/vm_nixos/configuration.nix

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 · Apply the change                                 (same machine you edited)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Build first if you want to compile without activating anything. Switch
builds and activates immediately.

  # test only — nothing activates
  sudo nixos-rebuild build --flake /etc/nixos#nixos

  # activate now
  sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake /etc/nixos#nixos

  # bad switch? roll back —
  sudo nixos-rebuild switch --rollback

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3 · Sync the edit to the other machine                                   (git)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Nothing propagates automatically — git push/pull is the only mechanism
between the two machines, same as any other repo.

  - On the machine you edited:
      git add -A && git commit -m "…" && git push
  - On the other machine:
      git pull --ff-only
      (Fails loudly instead of merge-committing if histories diverged — see
      the note below.)
  - Rebuild there too, only if you want this host's generation to change:
      sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake /etc/nixos#<attr>

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4 · Bump nixpkgs                                          (flake.lock is shared)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

One lock file pins both hosts to the same nixpkgs revision. Updating it is
a deliberate, cross-machine action — not a side effect of switching.

  - On one machine:
      cd /etc/nixos && nix flake update
      (No sudo — this just rewrites a file you own.)
  - git add flake.lock && git commit -m "update nixpkgs" && git push
  - On the other machine, before rebuilding it:
      git pull --ff-only
      (Skip this and the two hosts drift onto different nixpkgs revisions.)
  - Activate on each host whenever ready:
      sudo nixos-rebuild switch --flake /etc/nixos#<attr>

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
MENTAL MODEL
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  - Editing configuration.nix and running switch never touches flake.lock.
  - flake.lock only changes via nix flake update (or
    nix flake lock --update-input …).
  - Each host reads only its own hosts/<name>/configuration.nix — no
    cross-contamination.
  - `result` symlinks left by build/switch are harmless and already
    gitignored — rm them if they bother your prompt.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONCEPT — What --ff-only actually does
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Short for "fast-forward only." A fast-forward is possible only when your
local branch has made no commits the remote doesn't already have — git can
just slide your branch pointer forward to match, no merge required. With
--ff-only, if your local history has diverged instead, git pull refuses
outright rather than silently creating a merge commit. For a two-machine
setup, that's the safety you want: each machine should always just catch up
to what the other pushed, never auto-merge two different edits.
